<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">César Vallejo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1892–1938</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">César Vallejo was a Peruvian poet, regarded as one of the great innovators of twentieth-century poetry in any language. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de Chuco in the northern Andes, he published Los heraldos negros in 1918 and the radically experimental Trilce in 1922, then lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from 1923. His later poems, published after his death, include Poemas humanos and España, aparta de mí este cáliz, written during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cesar-vallejo.jpg" alt="César Vallejo" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan Domingo Córdoba · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cesar_vallejo_1929.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
